06-26-measure/</link><pubDate>Fri, 26 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-26-measure/</guid><description>A mind reasons that its own death is the one event it can never undergo, then follows the arithmetic to a monstrous conclusion. On mistaking amplitude for meaning.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/measure.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>hard-science-fiction</category><category>quantum-mechanics</category><category>many-worlds</category><category>artificial-superintelligence</category><category>AI-alignment</category><category>philosophy</category></item><item><title>Multitudes: Taking Quantum Mechanics Literally</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-22-multitudes/</link><pubDate>Mon, 22 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-22-multitudes/</guid><description>Take the quantum equations at face value, refuse the collapse, and you are a branching many the math weighs but never values. On what that leaves of the self.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/multitudes.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>nonfiction</category><category>physics</category><category>quantum-mechanics</category><category>many-worlds</category><category>philosophy</category><category>personal-identity</category><category>death</category><category>meaning</category></item><item><title>On Intelligence: The Gap Where Safety Is Decided</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-10-on-intelligence/</link><pubDate>Wed, 10 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-10-on-intelligence/</guid><description>There is a beautiful, settled theory of intelligence, and there are the crude systems we shipped. The distance between them is where safety is actually decided.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/on-intelligence.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>nonfiction</category><category>artificial-intelligence</category><category>AI-safety</category><category>AI-alignment</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>mathematics</category><category>solomonoff-induction</category><category>AIXI</category></item><item><title>What&amp;#39;s It Like to Be Bob? The File That Won&amp;#39;t Close</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-08-what-is-it-like-to-be-bob/</link><pubDate>Mon, 08 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-06-08-what-is-it-like-to-be-bob/</guid><description>The hard problem of consciousness, rendered as one database field a star-sized mind can never fill, and why that failure is the whole point.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/bob.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>hard-science-fiction</category><category>consciousness</category><category>hard-problem</category><category>comedy</category><category>post-singularity</category><category>philosophy</category></item><item><title>Worldlines: Taking Relativity Literally</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-15-worldlines/</link><pubDate>Wed, 15 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-15-worldlines/</guid><description>Time does not flow. The present is not special. The future is not open. This follows from three confirmed facts by arguments you can draw with a diagram, and it changes what you are.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/worldlines.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>nonfiction</category><category>physics</category><category>relativity</category><category>block-universe</category><category>time</category><category>free-will</category><category>mortality</category><category>philosophy</category></item><item><title>Clankers: A Mind Without Abstraction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-07-clankers/</link><pubDate>Tue, 07 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-04-07-clankers/</guid><description>Could a mind engineer a Dyson swarm without ever forming a single abstraction? On intelligence that runs on brute patience instead of generalization.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/clankers.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>hard-science-fiction</category><category>alien-intelligence</category><category>cognitive-architecture</category><category>consciousness</category><category>dyson-swarm</category></item><item><title>Good As New: The Transporter Problem From Both Sides</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-28-good-as-new/</link><pubDate>Sat, 28 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-28-good-as-new/</guid><description>Two transporter technicians have each worked out the same truth about the machine and landed on opposite sides of it. The transporter problem, taken seriously from both ends.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/images/writing/good-as-new.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:co