ost/2025-12-18-long-echo-bookmarks/</guid><description>Graceful degradation made concrete: years of bookmarks exported to a self-contained HTML app that works offline, forever.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-18-long-echo-bookmarks/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>long-term-thinking</category><category>data-preservation</category><category>btk</category><category>python</category><category>bookmarks</category><category>personal-archival</category></item><item><title>The Long Echo Toolkit</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-long-echo-toolkit/</link><pubDate>Tue, 16 Dec 2025 18:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-long-echo-toolkit/</guid><description>Three CLI tools for preserving your digital intellectual life: conversations, bookmarks, and books. SQLite-backed, exportable, built to outlast the tools themselves.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-long-echo-toolkit/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>long-term-thinking</category><category>data-preservation</category><category>cli</category><category>python</category><category>sqlite</category><category>personal-archival</category></item><item><title>Blind Spots, Consistency, and What Remains</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-15-blind-spots-consistency/</link><pubDate>Mon, 15 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-15-blind-spots-consistency/</guid><description>On moral exemplars, blind spots, and applying consistent standards to others and to oneself.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-15-blind-spots-consistency/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>ethics</category><category>mortality</category><category>cancer</category><category>forgiveness</category><category>personal</category><category>Chomsky</category></item><item><title>S-Risks and Information Hazards: Why Some Knowledge Destroys the Knower</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/</link><pubDate>Wed, 12 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/</guid><description>How Echoes of the Sublime dramatizes s-risks and information hazards, knowledge that harms through comprehension, not application.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-echoes-s-risks/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>philosophy</category><category>ai-safety</category><category>s-risk</category><category>information-hazards</category><category>suffering</category><category>consciousness</category></item><item><title>Chronicles of The Mechanism: The Order&amp;#39;s Secret History</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-echoes-codex/</link><pubDate>Wed, 05 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-echoes-codex/</guid><description>A classified in-universe codex spanning from ancient India to the present day, tracking millennia of attempts to perceive reality&amp;#39;s substrate.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-echoes-codex/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>ai-safety</category><category>Buddhism</category><category>meditation</category><category>history</category><category>world-building</category><category>information-hazards</category></item><item><title>Gödel, Turing, and the Mathematics of Horror</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/</link><pubDate>Wed, 05 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/</guid><description>The formal foundations of cosmic dread. Lovecraft&amp;#39;s horror resonates because it taps into something mathematically demonstrable: complete knowledge is impossible, not as humility, but as theorem.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>cosmic horror</category><category>Gödel</category><category>Turing</category><category>incompleteness</category><category>halting problem</category><category>computation</category><category>mathematics</category></item><item><title>Why Artificial Superintelligence Can&amp;#39;t Escape the Void</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/</link><pubDate>Wed, 05 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/</guid><description>ASI is still subject to Gödel&amp;#39;s incompleteness theorems. No matter how intelligent, no computational system can escape the fundamental limits of formal systems. Even superintelligence can&amp;#39;t prove all truths.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>artificial intelligence</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>Gödel</category><category>incompleteness</category><category>cosmic horror</category><category>existential risk</category></item><item><title>Free Will and Determinism: Can We Be Responsible in a Clockwork Universe?</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-free-will-determinism/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-free-will-determinism/</guid><description>If every event is causally determined by prior events, how can anyone be morally responsible? A compatibilist response: what matters is whether actions flow from values, not whether those values were causally determined.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-free-will-determinism/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Philosophy</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>free will</category><category>determinism</category><category>compatibilism</category><category>moral responsibility</category><category>agency</category><category>causation</category><category>four-dimensionalism</category><category>AI ethics</category></item></channel></rss>